WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.423
This is the technical deep dive
for Application Modernization,

3
00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:07.600
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.887
It's for architects,
application and data engineers,

5
00:00:10.887 --> 00:00:15.857
database administrators and system
security officers, the people who decide

6
00:00:15.857 --> 00:00:20.750
what each legacy application becomes, then
build it, move its data and prove it.

7
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.635
The book's idea is simple:
modernization is a portfolio

8
00:00:24.635 --> 00:00:29.037
decision made one application at
a time, not one giant rewrite.

9
00:00:29.037 --> 00:00:31.363
Part 1 is deciding and designing.

10
00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:33.638
Part 2 is governing and running.

11
00:00:33.638 --> 00:00:35.402
One note before we start:

12
00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:39.988
product versions here are as of
late October 2026, and they move.

13
00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:41.700
Let's begin with why.

14
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.837
Here's why this matters.

15
00:00:46.837 --> 00:00:50.873
Most federal I T money goes to
keeping existing systems running,

16
00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:53.613
and that share has barely
moved in six years.

17
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:57.782
G A O's list of the most critical
legacy systems is decades old,

18
00:00:57.782 --> 00:01:00.951
and most of those systems
lack complete plans.

19
00:01:00.951 --> 00:01:03.863
But those few systems are
only the top of the list.

20
00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:07.537
The real cost sits in thousands
of ordinary applications.

21
00:01:07.537 --> 00:01:12.913
The tempting answer is a grand rewrite,
and the record says it rarely ends well:

22
00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:17.449
value arrives only at the end, and
all the risk lands on one cut-over.

23
00:01:17.449 --> 00:01:21.875
Federal policy already asks for
small, useful pieces instead.

24
00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:23.550
So, Module 1.

25
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.313
Module 1 is about deciding.

26
00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:31.369
Before anyone picks a framework,

27
00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:35.025
every application needs a
decision about what it becomes.

28
00:01:35.025 --> 00:01:39.641
We'll look at the six dispositions, the
criteria behind them, how they're scored,

29
00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:42.166
the rules that turn a
score into a decision,

30
00:01:42.166 --> 00:01:46.050
and how owners and calibration
keep the whole thing honest.

31
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.089
The unit of decision is the application,
because that's where the owner,

32
00:01:54.089 --> 00:01:57.638
the data, the code and the
authorization boundary all meet.

33
00:01:57.638 --> 00:02:02.529
Each one gets one of six dispositions,
listed from cheapest to most expensive,

34
00:02:02.529 --> 00:02:07.650
and that order is the first rule: take the
cheapest one that meets the mission need.

35
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.050
Notice that retire and retain
aren't projects at all.

36
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.988
They're decisions, and they're
where the early savings are.

37
00:02:13.988 --> 00:02:17.863
The vendors' lists line up with
these, with one deliberate twist:

38
00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:21.738
replacing an application with a
product counts as retiring it.

39
00:02:21.738 --> 00:02:23.580
Next, what we score.

40
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.183
Six criteria drive the triage,

41
00:02:29.183 --> 00:02:32.668
and the good news is that you
already hold most of the data.

42
00:02:32.668 --> 00:02:36.348
Your security categorization,
configuration database,

43
00:02:36.348 --> 00:02:40.655
spending data, contracts and network
flows fill in nearly all of it.

44
00:02:40.655 --> 00:02:42.380
Two scoring rules matter.

45
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.780
Dependencies tell you the order to
move things in, not what they become.

46
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.004
And business logic buried in
the database counts twice,

47
00:02:50.004 --> 00:02:53.368
because it's both a skills
problem and a migration problem.

48
00:02:53.368 --> 00:02:57.729
Skills, by the way, are about
the next five years, not today.

49
00:02:57.729 --> 00:02:59.960
Now, how the scores combine.

50
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.555
The six criteria roll up into
two axes: how much the mission

51
00:03:07.555 --> 00:03:11.560
needs the application, and how
healthy it is to run and change.

52
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.985
The grid shows where each corner points.

53
00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:14.948
Low value?

54
00:03:14.948 --> 00:03:16.322
Retire it first.

55
00:03:16.322 --> 00:03:17.997
High value and healthy?

56
00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.473
Keep it, on purpose.

57
00:03:19.473 --> 00:03:21.372
High value and unhealthy?

58
00:03:21.372 --> 00:03:25.247
Fix the platform or the code,
whichever is the real problem.

59
00:03:25.247 --> 00:03:29.291
Every score also carries a flag
for how far you can trust it:

60
00:03:29.291 --> 00:03:31.397
measured, attested or assumed.

61
00:03:31.397 --> 00:03:34.061
Mostly assumed means no decision yet.

62
00:03:34.061 --> 00:03:38.948
And always prefer what's measured, like
usage logs, over what people believe.

63
00:03:38.948 --> 00:03:40.850
Next, the rules.

64
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.250
This is where scores become
a proposed decision.

65
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:50.465
Seven rules run in order,
and the first match wins,

66
00:03:50.465 --> 00:03:53.037
which keeps the cheapest answer on top.

67
00:03:53.037 --> 00:03:56.980
Retire, retain, rehost
and replatform come first,

68
00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.838
and only then refactor and rebuild.

69
00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:02.712
The seventh rule is what
makes this work at scale.

70
00:04:02.712 --> 00:04:05.170
High-impact systems headed for a rewrite,

71
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.350
high-impact systems with
open dependency questions,

72
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:12.469
applications that hold a master
record with many outside exchanges,

73
00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:15.288
and anything an owner
disputes, get a deep study.

74
00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:18.275
Everything else gets a decision
for the cost of scoring.

75
00:04:18.275 --> 00:04:20.906
So thousands of applications get decided,

76
00:04:20.906 --> 00:04:23.538
and the real analysis
goes where it counts.

77
00:04:23.538 --> 00:04:26.080
Next, the people who confirm it.

78
00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.518
The rubric proposes; owners confirm.

79
00:04:32.518 --> 00:04:35.946
Each application's owner gets
a fixed window to agree,

80
00:04:35.946 --> 00:04:40.855
or to contest with evidence, and a bad
score gets corrected, not argued around.

81
00:04:40.855 --> 00:04:45.106
What comes out is a decision
record: the disposition, the scores,

82
00:04:45.106 --> 00:04:49.030
the rule that fired, the owner's
agreement and a review date.

83
00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:53.817
That one record serves the architecture
plan, capital planning and the auditor.

84
00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:56.202
And before you run it
across the portfolio,

85
00:04:56.202 --> 00:05:00.493
calibrate it on a few dozen applications
your architects already know well.

86
00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:01.804
That's Module 1.

87
00:05:01.804 --> 00:05:03.340
Now, the target.

88
00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.499
Module 2 is about what the
changed applications land on,

89
00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:12.278
and how their data gets there.

90
00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:16.043
One shared target means the platform
team, the security team and

91
00:05:16.043 --> 00:05:19.952
the authorizing official review
one pattern instead of hundreds.

92
00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:24.490
We'll cover the layers, the support
clocks, contracts and configuration,

93
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.320
the strangler fig, and then the hardest
part of most refactors: the data.

94
00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:34.148
Here's the target.

95
00:05:34.148 --> 00:05:37.389
React front ends that only call A P I's.

96
00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:40.038
A thin Node layer where
a front end needs one,

97
00:05:40.038 --> 00:05:44.382
and a gateway that also acts as the
switch for incremental replacement.

98
00:05:44.382 --> 00:05:49.302
Java and Spring Boot services that each
publish a contract and own their data.

99
00:05:49.302 --> 00:05:51.969
And everything in
containers on Kubernetes.

100
00:05:51.969 --> 00:05:56.258
These are mainstream choices with big
labor pools and long support windows,

101
00:05:56.258 --> 00:05:59.117
because these business
rules will live for decades.

102
00:05:59.117 --> 00:06:02.098
And they're the program's
design, not a federal rule.

103
00:06:02.098 --> 00:06:06.267
An agency built on .NET can keep
the structure and swap the list.

104
00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:08.070
Next, the clocks.

105
00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.270
Versions are a snapshot.

106
00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.545
Support windows are the real story.

107
00:06:15.545 --> 00:06:18.787
Java's long-term releases
are supported for years,

108
00:06:18.787 --> 00:06:21.870
so build on one and move
to the next within a year.

109
00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.363
Spring Boot is shorter: each recent
generation gets only about thirteen

110
00:06:26.363 --> 00:06:30.632
months of open-source support, and
one generation has already run out.

111
00:06:30.632 --> 00:06:33.396
Node and Kubernetes keep
their own clocks too.

112
00:06:33.396 --> 00:06:36.132
So upgrades can't be left to each project.

113
00:06:36.132 --> 00:06:40.170
Build the cadence into the platform
and fund it as recurring work,

114
00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.833
or today's modernized portfolio
becomes tomorrow's legacy.

115
00:06:43.833 --> 00:06:46.850
Next, the disciplines
that hold it together.

116
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.300
Three disciplines hold
the architecture together.

117
00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.162
First, the contract comes before the code.

118
00:06:56.162 --> 00:07:00.006
Teams agree on the Open A P
I document, generate from it,

119
00:07:00.006 --> 00:07:04.024
test against it, and publish
it so the next team reuses it.

120
00:07:04.024 --> 00:07:06.500
Second, keep the thin layers thin.

121
00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.600
Node serves the front end's needs,

122
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.800
and business rules stay in the
services, where they're tested once.

123
00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.075
Third, configuration comes
from outside the code.

124
00:07:16.075 --> 00:07:18.607
The best test is whether
you could publish the

125
00:07:18.607 --> 00:07:21.538
code tomorrow without
exposing a single credential.

126
00:07:21.538 --> 00:07:24.790
Next, how a refactor actually happens.

127
00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.078
A refactor happens
through the strangler fig.

128
00:07:31.078 --> 00:07:33.907
Put a façade in front of
the legacy application,

129
00:07:33.907 --> 00:07:37.110
move one function at a time
behind the same contract,

130
00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:41.877
retire the old system when nothing depends
on it, and finally remove the façade.

131
00:07:41.877 --> 00:07:45.177
You can roll back right up until
the legacy tables are gone.

132
00:07:45.177 --> 00:07:49.121
Before you commit, check that
requests can actually be intercepted,

133
00:07:49.121 --> 00:07:51.303
and that there's a plan for shared data.

134
00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:53.877
Those questions belong in the deep study.

135
00:07:53.877 --> 00:07:58.640
And for security, two NIST guides
cover microservices and containers.

136
00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.790
They're guidance, not requirements.

137
00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:02.390
Now, the data.

138
00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:09.686
Code can be replaced a piece at
a time, but data moves once,

139
00:08:09.686 --> 00:08:13.385
and the move isn't done until
you can prove nothing was lost.

140
00:08:13.385 --> 00:08:17.431
So the program treats it as
three jobs: convert the schema,

141
00:08:17.431 --> 00:08:21.915
move the rows, and prove the result,
looping back on any mismatch.

142
00:08:21.915 --> 00:08:25.476
Choosing the engine is usually
settled by what you already run.

143
00:08:25.476 --> 00:08:29.626
And remember that the engine also decides
which conversion tools you can use.

144
00:08:29.626 --> 00:08:35.720
If the target is SQL Server, arrive
already on Book 6's modern sign-in pattern.

145
00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.310
Next, where conversions go wrong.

146
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.188
Conversion tools do most of the work,

147
00:08:44.188 --> 00:08:47.523
but the dangerous conversions
are the ones that succeed.

148
00:08:47.523 --> 00:08:52.523
Map an Oracle date to a plain date
and you silently lose the time of day.

149
00:08:52.523 --> 00:08:56.022
Treat money as floating
point and you lose exactness.

150
00:08:56.022 --> 00:09:00.172
Oracle treats an empty string
as null, and the targets don't.

151
00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:04.673
And sequences need to move past the
highest key before the first new record.

152
00:09:04.673 --> 00:09:07.923
Stored procedures and
packages are the long pole.

153
00:09:07.923 --> 00:09:13.460
Tools emulate them, but the program treats
every emulation call as debt to pay down.

154
00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.840
Next, the tools themselves.

155
00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:25.327
Here are the conversion tools as of October
2026, as examples, not endorsements.

156
00:09:25.327 --> 00:09:27.027
Three facts shape your plan.

157
00:09:27.027 --> 00:09:31.439
Only A W S tools convert
Db2 to PostgreSQL.

158
00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:35.828
Microsoft's assistant targets
only SQL Server and Azure SQL.

159
00:09:35.828 --> 00:09:40.017
And the newer AI-assisted conversion
sends your source code to a model,

160
00:09:40.017 --> 00:09:43.990
which puts that model's endpoint
inside your migration's boundary.

161
00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.659
Treat whatever it produces as a draft that

162
00:09:46.659 --> 00:09:49.328
a person reviews and the validation tests.

163
00:09:49.328 --> 00:09:51.766
So pick the engine with
these limits in view.

164
00:09:51.766 --> 00:09:53.850
Next, proving the move.

165
00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.010
A migration is finished when
you can prove the target holds

166
00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:04.350
the same facts as the source,
and no single check can do that.

167
00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:09.553
So use three with different blind spots:
row counts, a checksum over every row,

168
00:10:09.553 --> 00:10:13.600
and business totals computed
separately in each database.

169
00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.412
The third one matters most.

170
00:10:15.412 --> 00:10:18.326
If your transform drops
the time from every date,

171
00:10:18.326 --> 00:10:22.913
the checksum still matches perfectly,
because it ran after the same mistake.

172
00:10:22.913 --> 00:10:25.351
The business totals
don't share that mistake.

173
00:10:25.351 --> 00:10:28.225
Pick totals the program office recognizes.

174
00:10:28.225 --> 00:10:30.440
Next, the switch itself.

175
00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:37.131
There are three ways to
cut over: all at once,

176
00:10:37.131 --> 00:10:40.493
in phases, or by running
both systems in parallel.

177
00:10:40.493 --> 00:10:42.378
All three end at the same gate:

178
00:10:42.378 --> 00:10:46.070
three clean validation results
and a rehearsed way back.

179
00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:51.393
Phases and parallel runs mean both systems
live side by side for a while, and here's

180
00:10:51.393 --> 00:10:56.554
the rule that matters most: never let
the application write to both databases.

181
00:10:56.554 --> 00:10:58.798
Those writes drift apart silently.

182
00:10:58.798 --> 00:11:04.285
Instead, one system owns each table,
and changes are replicated from the log.

183
00:11:04.285 --> 00:11:05.599
That's Module 2.

184
00:11:05.599 --> 00:11:07.320
Next, Part 2.

185
00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.620
Module 3 begins Part 2,
governing and running.

186
00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.758
It's about keeping all of this true
across hundreds of delivery teams.

187
00:11:18.758 --> 00:11:21.882
We'll cover where AI fits
and how agents are fenced in,

188
00:11:21.882 --> 00:11:26.096
which controls the architecture
carries and the evidence they leave,

189
00:11:26.096 --> 00:11:30.020
and the review board and the
four gates every change passes.

190
00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:36.184
Treat a model like any other component:

191
00:11:36.184 --> 00:11:40.483
something behind an interface, whose
output gets checked, not obeyed.

192
00:11:40.483 --> 00:11:44.982
AI shows up in four ways, ordered by how
much each can change, from a drafting

193
00:11:44.982 --> 00:11:49.333
feature that changes nothing to an
agent that can act through its tools.

194
00:11:49.333 --> 00:11:53.968
For retrieval, one rule matters: it
searches with the user's own permissions,

195
00:11:53.968 --> 00:11:56.808
so it can't surface anything
they couldn't open.

196
00:11:56.808 --> 00:12:01.217
And everything calls models through
one provider-neutral interface,

197
00:12:01.217 --> 00:12:06.007
so swapping providers is configuration,
not code, and every call is recorded.

198
00:12:06.007 --> 00:12:07.860
Next, the agent.

199
00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.972
An agent's risk is what it
can do, not what it says.

200
00:12:14.972 --> 00:12:18.353
And because hidden instructions
in a document can steer it,

201
00:12:18.353 --> 00:12:20.423
you can't count on the model refusing.

202
00:12:20.423 --> 00:12:23.598
So design for a steered agent
that still can't do harm.

203
00:12:23.598 --> 00:12:28.274
Every agent has an identity and a
human sponsor, a short list of allowed

204
00:12:28.274 --> 00:12:32.547
tools enforced outside the model,
a step limit and an audit trail.

205
00:12:32.547 --> 00:12:35.860
The big one: no agent writes
to a system of record.

206
00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:40.323
It proposes, a person approves,
and the service applies the change.

207
00:12:40.323 --> 00:12:44.697
And the Model Context Protocol connects
tools, but it isn't a guardrail.

208
00:12:44.697 --> 00:12:46.590
Next, compliance.

209
00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.282
Compliance is cheapest
when the platform carries

210
00:12:53.282 --> 00:12:56.365
the controls and the pipeline
produces the evidence.

211
00:12:56.365 --> 00:13:01.240
Implement a control once in the target,
and every application on it inherits it.

212
00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:05.857
The diagram shows where the controls
land, from design through operation,

213
00:13:05.857 --> 00:13:09.415
with everything flowing into
one authorization record.

214
00:13:09.415 --> 00:13:13.292
The table shows the control families
application work touches most,

215
00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:15.265
and the evidence each should leave.

216
00:13:15.265 --> 00:13:19.609
And here's the point for triage:
keeping an application on unsupported

217
00:13:19.609 --> 00:13:23.728
software with no plan is a compliance
finding, not a neutral choice.

218
00:13:23.728 --> 00:13:26.230
Next, three design inputs.

219
00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:34.068
Three things usually get reviewed at
the end, and should come in at design.

220
00:13:34.068 --> 00:13:37.831
Secure development: the federal
attestation memo was rescinded,

221
00:13:37.831 --> 00:13:41.316
but you're the producer of your
own code, so build to the secure

222
00:13:41.316 --> 00:13:45.218
development framework and let the
pipeline generate the evidence.

223
00:13:45.218 --> 00:13:50.353
Privacy: the program names the events
that reopen privacy work, like a new

224
00:13:50.353 --> 00:13:55.317
data store, a new flow or an AI feature,
so the privacy office hears early.

225
00:13:55.317 --> 00:13:57.439
And records: retiring a system,

226
00:13:57.439 --> 00:14:01.429
or dropping history in a
migration, is a records decision.

227
00:14:01.429 --> 00:14:03.370
Next, who decides.

228
00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.776
No rule requires an architecture review
board, but several require what one

229
00:14:11.776 --> 00:14:14.140
produces: an enterprise architecture,

230
00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:17.170
a fit check for each system,
and controlled change.

231
00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.308
The key design choice is
one decider per decision.

232
00:14:20.308 --> 00:14:22.620
The board reviews; it doesn't vote.

233
00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.883
The chief architect owns the standards,
the application owner owns the disposition,

234
00:14:27.883 --> 00:14:32.995
the CIO approves what gets bought, and only
the authorizing official accepts risk.

235
00:14:32.995 --> 00:14:37.978
The board works through versioned artifacts:
a technology list with support dates,

236
00:14:37.978 --> 00:14:40.795
runnable patterns, and
short decision records.

237
00:14:40.795 --> 00:14:42.580
Next, the gates.

238
00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:49.130
Every changed application
passes four gates.

239
00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:52.548
The first two, the disposition
and the design review,

240
00:14:52.548 --> 00:14:56.442
are meetings, because they need
judgment and happen rarely.

241
00:14:56.442 --> 00:15:00.738
The last two, the pull request and
the release, run many times a day,

242
00:15:00.738 --> 00:15:04.467
so they're automated in the
repository and the pipeline.

243
00:15:04.467 --> 00:15:06.355
That ratio is deliberate.

244
00:15:06.355 --> 00:15:09.817
Run a daily gate as a meeting
and teams stop running it.

245
00:15:09.817 --> 00:15:12.968
Each gate leaves its own
evidence, kept by system,

246
00:15:12.968 --> 00:15:18.243
and one rule holds throughout: nothing
counts as done unless it carries its proof.

247
00:15:18.243 --> 00:15:19.990
That's Module 3.

248
00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:25.702
Module 4 makes it concrete.

249
00:15:25.702 --> 00:15:29.532
We'll look at the small reference
implementation that ships with the book,

250
00:15:29.532 --> 00:15:31.603
and be honest about what its tests prove.

251
00:15:31.603 --> 00:15:34.199
Then we'll place the
work in the frameworks

252
00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:37.464
architecture offices already
use, TOGAF and Zachman.

253
00:15:37.464 --> 00:15:40.370
And we'll finish with running
the portfolio in waves,

254
00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.170
and the decisions that
belong to each owner.

255
00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.708
Arguments about architecture
are cheap; code that runs isn't.

256
00:15:50.708 --> 00:15:55.531
So the book ships a small slice of
the target: a case-management service,

257
00:15:55.531 --> 00:15:59.107
a React front end, a migration
tool and a triage agent.

258
00:15:59.107 --> 00:16:03.112
It's deliberately small, with
synthetic data and no sign-in,

259
00:16:03.112 --> 00:16:06.533
so read it as an illustration,
not production code.

260
00:16:06.533 --> 00:16:09.490
Notice that the service
owns its database schema,

261
00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:12.520
and the migration tool
loads into that same schema.

262
00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.777
Two choices differ from the target,

263
00:16:14.777 --> 00:16:19.370
an older Java and an older contract
version, and the book says so plainly.

264
00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:21.970
Next, what the code demonstrates.

265
00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.282
Three parts show the
patterns most directly.

266
00:16:28.282 --> 00:16:32.288
The service's contract is checked
against the code in both directions,

267
00:16:32.288 --> 00:16:35.120
so the two can't drift apart
without a failed build.

268
00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:39.276
The migration tool handles real
legacy habits, like padded text

269
00:16:39.276 --> 00:16:43.432
and placeholder dates, and refuses
to guess at a value it can't map.

270
00:16:43.432 --> 00:16:46.211
And the agent can't even
start without an owner,

271
00:16:46.211 --> 00:16:48.844
and can only ever record
a pending proposal.

272
00:16:48.844 --> 00:16:51.660
Best of all, the tests
break things on purpose,

273
00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:55.920
to prove each check and guardrail
catches the failure it exists for.

274
00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.960
Next, the honest part.

275
00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.398
Here's what was actually
verified, and what wasn't.

276
00:17:04.398 --> 00:17:07.331
The service, the contract
and the migration

277
00:17:07.331 --> 00:17:10.672
checks all pass against
a real PostgreSQL engine.

278
00:17:10.672 --> 00:17:14.634
The guardrails hold against a scripted
model, though not a real one.

279
00:17:14.634 --> 00:17:16.235
But two things are unproven.

280
00:17:16.235 --> 00:17:20.595
The migration has never run against
a real Db2 or Oracle database,

281
00:17:20.595 --> 00:17:24.147
only a stand-in, and the
containers were never started.

282
00:17:24.147 --> 00:17:27.960
So test against a copy of your
real source before relying on it.

283
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.933
A production version
would add real sign-in,

284
00:17:30.933 --> 00:17:34.485
an agent identity, a secrets
vault and signed builds.

285
00:17:34.485 --> 00:17:36.620
Next, the frameworks.

286
00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:43.136
Many architecture offices think in TOGAF,

287
00:17:43.136 --> 00:17:45.982
so the book maps its work
onto TOGAF's phases.

288
00:17:45.982 --> 00:17:49.932
First, the requirement itself:
policy asks for an enterprise

289
00:17:49.932 --> 00:17:53.882
architecture with a baseline,
a target and a transition plan.

290
00:17:53.882 --> 00:17:56.170
It doesn't require any framework.

291
00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:59.061
Triage is the business
baseline and the options,

292
00:17:59.061 --> 00:18:03.758
the reference architecture is the target,
and the waves are the migration plan.

293
00:18:03.758 --> 00:18:06.326
The gates are implementation governance,

294
00:18:06.326 --> 00:18:09.408
and re-scoring the portfolio
starts the next cycle.

295
00:18:09.408 --> 00:18:14.045
The point is to file what you already
produce, not to create new deliverables.

296
00:18:14.045 --> 00:18:15.730
Next, Zachman.

297
00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.752
If TOGAF tells you the order of the work,

298
00:18:21.752 --> 00:18:24.805
Zachman tells you whether
you've described all of it.

299
00:18:24.805 --> 00:18:29.243
It's a six-by-six grid of questions and
perspectives, and it isn't a method.

300
00:18:29.243 --> 00:18:31.744
The table shows which
cells the book fills,

301
00:18:31.744 --> 00:18:35.604
from the executive view of the
portfolio down to the running code.

302
00:18:35.604 --> 00:18:40.713
Reading it by column shows two gaps:
locations and networks, which other books

303
00:18:40.713 --> 00:18:45.980
cover, and timing detail like batch windows,
which belongs in each design review.

304
00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:48.905
An empty cell is a question, not a defect.

305
00:18:48.905 --> 00:18:50.330
Now, running it.

306
00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.655
A triage gives you a list, not a plan.

307
00:18:56.655 --> 00:19:00.758
Waves turn it into one: batches
that move through the same steps,

308
00:19:00.758 --> 00:19:02.730
each leaving the next cheaper.

309
00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:05.327
The portfolio doesn't
get its own schedule;

310
00:19:05.327 --> 00:19:10.224
it runs inside the program's existing
phases and gates, from scoring everything,

311
00:19:10.224 --> 00:19:12.079
through building the factory,

312
00:19:12.079 --> 00:19:15.492
a pilot and the main waves, to
retiring what was replaced.

313
00:19:15.492 --> 00:19:19.796
Keep the pilot small, but include
one genuinely hard application,

314
00:19:19.796 --> 00:19:22.267
so the first real wave isn't a surprise.

315
00:19:22.267 --> 00:19:25.843
And size later waves by what the
factory has actually delivered.

316
00:19:25.843 --> 00:19:28.270
Next, the engine behind it.

317
00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:36.292
Group applications that share a
database into the same wave, and keep a

318
00:19:36.292 --> 00:19:40.858
ranked backlog several waves deep, with
only the next one planned in detail.

319
00:19:40.858 --> 00:19:45.868
The factory, meaning the platform,
patterns, pipelines and runbooks, is judged

320
00:19:45.868 --> 00:19:50.644
by one test: the fifth wave should cost
less per application than the first.

321
00:19:50.644 --> 00:19:53.632
Report progress from records, not plans.

322
00:19:53.632 --> 00:19:58.045
Fund it wave by wave, so savings from
retirements pay for the next one.

323
00:19:58.045 --> 00:20:01.142
And here's the honest rule:
cost avoided is only a

324
00:20:01.142 --> 00:20:04.795
forecast until the old system
is actually switched off.

325
00:20:04.795 --> 00:20:06.850
Let's close with decisions.

326
00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.925
Every decision here has one owner.

327
00:20:12.925 --> 00:20:17.050
Leadership approves the triage
rules once and settles disputes.

328
00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:19.975
Application owners decide dispositions.

329
00:20:19.975 --> 00:20:24.000
The chief architect owns the
technology list and keeps it current.

330
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:27.400
The security and privacy
officials set the controls.

331
00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:32.437
The CIO approves what's bought, and only
the authorizing official accepts risk.

332
00:20:32.437 --> 00:20:36.497
You can start Monday without a
new platform: build the inventory,

333
00:20:36.497 --> 00:20:40.710
calibrate the rubric, publish the
list with end dates, ask for three

334
00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:45.612
validation results before any cut-over,
and keep agents from writing records.

335
00:20:45.612 --> 00:20:49.837
Decide one application at a time,
and let the gates write the evidence.

336
00:20:49.837 --> 00:20:50.650
Thank you.
